[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I want to make sure about one thing: the Yosida-Hewitt decomposition [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mand that $\ell_1$ is 1-complemented. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Yosida-Hewitt decomposition (1952) states that every bounded finitely [0m
[38;2;124;124;124madditive measure $\mu$ on a $\sigma$-algebra can be uniquely decomposed as $\mu [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= \mu_c + \mu_p$ where $\mu_c$ is countably additive and $\mu_p$ is purely [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfinitely additive. On $\mathbb{N}$ with the power set $\sigma$-algebra, the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcountably additive measures correspond exactly to $\ell_1$ (via $\mu_c(A) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\sum_{n \in A} a_n$ where $a_n = \mu_c(\{n\})$). The map $\mu \mapsto \mu_c$ is [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma projection of norm 1 (since $\|\mu\| = \|\mu_c\| + \|\mu_p\|$ in the Yosida-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHewitt decomposition, the total variation norm is additive). So $\ell_1$ is 1-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcomplemented in $ba(\mathbb{N}) = \ell_\infty^*$. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me write the complete proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I realize I should double-check: is the norm additive in the YH [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdecomposition? The Yosida-Hewitt theorem says $\mu = \mu_c + \mu_p$ with $\mu_c [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\perp \mu_p$ (mutually singular in some sense). For the total variation norm, $|[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\mu| = |\mu_c| + |\mu_p|$, so $\|\mu\| = \|\mu_c\| + \|\mu_p\|$. Yes, this is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcorrect. So the projection has norm 1. ✓[0m

